    <statement><p>Find the intersection of the line and plane:</p>
<p><m>4y-2x-4z=-18, \qquad \mathbf{r}(t)=\left\lt 0,-3,2\right&gt;+t\left\lt 3,0,-1\right&gt;</m></p>
<p><m>P=(</m> <fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,<fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0002"/>,<fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0003"/><m>)</m></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x=3t,\qquad y=-3,\qquad z=2-t</m>



We substitute the parametric equations of the plane and solve for <m>t</m>, to find the 
value of <m>t</m> for which <m>\left(x,y,z\right)</m>  lies on the plane. We obtain:



<m>4y-2x-4z=-18</m>



<m>-2\cdot 3t+\left(-12\right)+\left(-4\right)\mathopen{}\left(2-t\right)=-18</m>



<m>t=-1</m>



We find the coordinates of the point <m>P</m> of intersection by substituting <m>t=-1</m> 
in the parametric equations of the line. We obtain:



<m>x=0 - 1\cdot (3)=-3 \qquad y=-3 - 1\cdot (0)=-3 \qquad z=2 - 1\cdot (-1)=3</m>



That is



<m>P=\left( -3,-3,3 \right)</m>




</solution>
